    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d79">

                <pb facs="0085" n="73" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg73"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/19498: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Egypt</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kirk</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-02-27T20:00:00-04:00">February 27,
                            1942—8 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>168. A paraphrase of your 273, February 16, 11 a.m., was transmitted to the War
                    Department, which desires you to be informed in effect that the Chiefs of Staff
                    and the Combined Planning Committee are cognizant of the situation in the Middle
                    East. Every consideration is being given to the requirements of that area
                    consistent with the needs of other vital areas and with the means
                        available.<anchor n="15a" xml:id="d79-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d79-f1" n="15a" xml:id="d79-f1-content">
                <p>Concerning the place of Egypt and the Middle East in War Department planning in
                    1942, see Maurice Matloff and Edwin M. Snell, <hi rendition="#italic">Strategic
                        Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1941–1942</hi>, in the series <hi
                        rendition="#italic">United States Army in World War II: The War
                        Department</hi> (Washington, Government Printing Office, 1953), particularly
                    pp. 165–168, 198–202, 226–227, 244–255, 277, and 297–298.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
